To make ESLint ignore all rules for a single line, you can use a comment to disable the rules for that specific line. Here are the two common ways to do this:

1. **Using `// eslint-disable-next-line`**:
   This is the most common method. Place the comment at the end of the line you want to ignore.

   ```javascript
   console.log('This line will not follow any rules'); // eslint-disable-next-line
   ```

2. **Using `/* eslint-disable */` and `/* eslint-enable */`**:
   You can also disable rules for a block of code. This is useful if you want to ignore multiple lines or a specific section of code.

   ```javascript
   /* eslint-disable */
   console.log('This line will not follow any rules');
   console.log('Neither will this one');
   /* eslint-enable */
   ```

### Example

Here’s an example of how you might use these in a JavaScript file:

```javascript
// This line will not follow any rules
console.log('This line will not follow any rules'); // eslint-disable-next-line

/* eslint-disable */
console.log('This line will not follow any rules');
console.log('Neither will this one');
/* eslint-enable */

// This line will follow all rules
console.log('This line will follow all rules');
```

### Important Notes

- **Use with Caution**: Disabling rules for a single line or block of code can make your code harder to maintain. It’s generally better to fix the issues that ESLint is pointing out.
- **Consistency**: If you find yourself disabling rules frequently, it might be worth revisiting your code to see if there are underlying issues that need to be addressed.

By using these methods, you can selectively ignore ESLint rules for specific lines or blocks of code.
